{
  "global_card_id": "divergence",
  "global_canonical": "Divergence",
  "type": "merged_concept",
  "school": "Indicator Based",
  "aliases": [
    "MACD Divergence"
  ],
  "definition": "Divergence is a discrepancy between price direction and an oscillator (RSI, MACD, MFI) at the same swing points. Regular divergence (bullish/bearish) signals weakening momentum and potential reversal: price makes a new extreme while the oscillator fails to confirm. Hidden divergence signals trend continuation. Divergence warns of momentum loss rather than guaranteeing an automatic reversal, and requires confirmation from price action, market structure, or key levels.",
  "definition_per_source": {
    "SMC-Strong-Weak-Supplement": "Divergence occurs when price and an oscillator (RSI, MACD) move in opposite directions. Regular divergence (bullish/bearish) signals potential reversal—price makes lower low/higher high while the oscillator makes higher low/lower high. Hidden divergence signals continuation—price makes higher low/lower high while the oscillator makes lower low/higher high.",
    "SMC-Supplement-PLv-X125": "A discrepancy between price direction and RSI direction. Regular divergence (bearish: price higher high, RSI lower high; bullish: price lower low, RSI higher low) signals potential reversals. Hidden divergence signals continuation. It indicates weakening momentum, not automatic reversal; hidden divergences almost always lead to continuation, while regular ones do not always reverse."
  },
  "identification_rules": [
    "Regular bullish divergence: price forms a lower swing low while the oscillator forms a higher swing low over the same period. (PLv-X125 adds: a double bottom or flat lows on price with a higher low on RSI also qualifies.)",
    "Regular bearish divergence: price forms a higher swing high while the oscillator forms a lower swing high over the same period.",
    "Hidden bullish divergence (uptrend continuation): price forms a higher swing low while the oscillator forms a lower swing low.",
    "Hidden bearish divergence (downtrend continuation): price forms a lower swing high while the oscillator forms a higher swing high.",
    "PLv-X125 stresses identifying current market structure first (HH/HL = uptrend, LH/LL = downtrend) before classifying divergence type.",
    "Compare consecutive, clearly separated swing highs/lows on price against the corresponding oscillator peaks/troughs at those same points; use two clearly separated swings to avoid noise.",
    "Divergence can be identified on oscillators such as RSI, MACD (line or histogram), MFI, etc.",
    "PLv-X125 (RSI-specific): track whether RSI peaks/troughs are above, within, or below the 70/30 range — a divergence where RSI drops from outside the 70/30 zone back into it signals significant momentum loss and higher-probability setups.",
    "PLv-X125: wait for RSI to exit overbought/oversold territory and re-enter the 70–30 range before treating the signal as actionable.",
    "PLv-X125: wait for a multi-touch divergence pattern with a clear RSI slope rather than acting on a single divergence point; multiple divergences can form before a reversal occurs.",
    "Check higher time frames first; a divergence on the daily or 1-hour chart outweighs one on the 5-minute or 15-minute chart.",
    "PLv-X125: confirm prior oscillator behavior was in confluence with the trend — the divergence is the point where the oscillator deviates from what price structure suggests.",
    "PLv-X125: look for momentum loss in price action (consolidation, smaller candles) as a precondition before checking for divergence.",
    "Do not enter on divergence alone; SMC-Strong-Weak prefers confirmation via price action, market structure, or S/R, while PLv-X125 requires a price action rejection pattern (double bottom, engulfing) or RSI breaking the 50 level.",
    "Strong-Weak: prefer manual identification of significant swings; if using automated detection, adjust pivot lookback settings to balance timeliness and false positives."
  ],
  "common_mistakes": [
    "Assuming divergence leads to an immediate reversal without confirmation from price action, market structure, or support/resistance; entering the moment it appears.",
    "Assuming every regular divergence guarantees a reversal — it only signals trend weakness and needs confirmation.",
    "PLv-X125: entering before RSI re-enters the 70–30 range, or while all RSI peaks are still above 70 (momentum still strong, divergence premature).",
    "PLv-X125: trading the first minor/single divergence point instead of waiting for a clear multi-touch pattern with an obvious slope.",
    "Trading divergence on lower time frames (1m/5m) expecting a large swing reversal without referencing higher time frames.",
    "Treating regular divergence as more significant than hidden divergence — PLv-X125 emphasizes hidden divergences (continuation) outweigh regular ones.",
    "Confusing which swing points to compare (e.g., using highs for bullish or lows for bearish), leading to misidentification of regular vs hidden divergence.",
    "Failing to identify current market structure before classifying divergence type.",
    "Treating weakly connected or overlapping price swings as valid divergence setups.",
    "Confusing MACD line divergence with MACD histogram divergence (Strong-Weak).",
    "Confusing a break of market structure (price failing to make a new HH) with divergence, which specifically requires price to make a new extreme while the oscillator diverges (PLv-X125).",
    "Relying solely on oscillator overbought/oversold levels for signals instead of confirming a momentum shift via divergence.",
    "Using divergence in isolation without other confluences (price action patterns, Fibonacci levels, market structure).",
    "Relying on automatic divergence indicators / ignoring pivot-lookback settings (Strong-Weak), or relying on idealized textbook illustrations instead of real charts (PLv-X125).",
    "PLv-X125: placing stops at arbitrary levels instead of beyond the previous swing; trading divergences in flat/ranging markets instead of trending markets with clear directional swings."
  ],
  "trading_implication": "Both sources treat divergence as a leading momentum signal requiring confirmation. Regular divergence warns of weakening momentum and possible reversal (bullish = enter long/exit short; bearish = enter short/exit long), used to time counter-trend entries toward key S/R; hidden divergence confirms trend continuation for high-probability trend-aligned entries. SMC-Strong-Weak recommends confirming with price action, market structure, or S/R and using higher time frames, noting aggressive oscillator settings produce false signals. SMC-Supplement-PLv-X125 is more procedural for RSI: highest-probability entries occur as RSI exits the 70/30 extreme and re-enters the normal range, with the first in-range peak/trough marking entry; it emphasizes hidden divergences outweigh regular ones, divergence at significant levels (Fibonacci Gold Zone, key S/R) with price action confirmation, and placing stops beyond the previous swing.",
  "merge_notes": "Both schools agree on core definitions and the four divergence types. Key differences: PLv-X125 is RSI-centric and adds a detailed procedural framework (70/30 range tracking, multi-touch slope requirement, structure-first classification, stop placement beyond swing), and explicitly ranks hidden divergence above regular. Strong-Weak is more oscillator-agnostic (RSI/MACD line vs histogram/MFI) and focuses on manual vs automated detection and pivot-lookback settings. Neither contradicts the other; PLv-X125 simply provides stricter entry/timing rules.",
